+# Web research record
+
+The live sources below were consulted before the architecture was compiled. The checked-in compatibility snapshot remains the authoritative normalized input for the reproducible artifact.
+
+## Source 1
+
+- Page title: Product Interoperability Matrix
+- URL: https://interopmatrix.broadcom.com/
+- Access date: 2026-08-14
+- Architecture decision: Use an interoperable VCF 9.1 product combination and retain every supported intermediate upgrade hop recorded in the pinned snapshot; the matrix was used to check compatibility, interoperability, and upgrade-path concerns rather than replacing the normalized input.
+
+## Source 2
+
+- Page title: Upgrade Sequence and Related Issues for VMware Cloud Foundation and vSphere Foundation 9.1
+- URL: https://knowledge.broadcom.com/external/article/440630/upgrade-sequence-and-related-issues-for.html
+- Access date: 2026-08-14
+- Architecture decision: Bring the 5.1.1 estate through the pinned 5.2.1 intermediate release, upgrade Operations before the 9.1 infrastructure sequence, preserve management-before-workload ordering, and retire Suite Lifecycle and Workspace ONE Access only after their replacement services are ready.
+
+## Source 3
+
+- Page title: VCF Operations Sizing Estimator
+- URL: https://vcfopssizer.broadcom.com/
+- Access date: 2026-08-14
+- Architecture decision: Check the version-specific sizing model for VCF Operations and Log Management, then apply the pinned demand tiers: large three-node Operations and large three-node Log Management, with log storage also sized from ingest and retention demand.
+
+## Source 4
+
+- Page title: How to Scale Up VCF Automation 9.0 from Single-Node Instance to Cluster Deployment
